course: everyday
lesson: 363
title: Mean messages, fake ages and sharing photos
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: In the class group chat in Vancouver someone posted a silly photo of Daniel mid-sneeze. Zack did not post it — he only added three crying-laughing faces like everyone else. Daniel saw the chat that night and cried; his mum told the school, and the teacher talked to the whole class. Zack felt sick, because he knew his laughs had been part of it. He said sorry to Daniel face to face and left the chat when it turned mean again.
explain: Ellie played her game for hours instead of doing homework. The first week the teacher did not collect it, and Ellie thought «easy». The second week she copied answers on the bus. The third week the teacher sent a note home, and then asked Mum and Dad to come in. After the meeting there was a row, the game was gone for a week, and Ellie did not speak to Mum all evening. The next day she said sorry, and they set a timer: homework first, then games.
explain: Online, it is easy to think small things do not count — one emoji, one fake name, one password passed on. But laughing along is joining in, pretending to be someone else can fool and hurt people, and a password shared lets a stranger act as your friend.
explain: Consequences differ: sometimes nothing, sometimes a note home, sometimes parents called to school. That it went fine before does not mean it will again — or that it was ever right.
fact: Adding a laughing emoji to a mean post is joining in, not just watching.
fact: A child who is laughed at online often feels it everywhere, because the phone comes home with them.
fact: Downloading games or apps without asking can cost money or bring in harmful software.
fact: Pretending to be someone else online can fool and upset real people.
fact: Chain messages that say something bad will happen if you do not pass them on are untrue, but they can scare younger children.
fact: Taking and sharing a photo of someone without asking, including a teacher, can get you into serious trouble at school, such as being sent to the head teacher.
fact: A password is private; giving a friend's password to someone else lets them pretend to be your friend.
fact: Using a parent's account to buy something without asking is taking their money.
fact: When parents are called to school, it is to solve a problem together, not to shame the child.
fact: Leaving a chat that has turned mean, or telling an adult, is a brave and sensible choice.
exercise: Zack did not post the photo; he only added laughing faces. Why was that still wrong? | Laughing along is joining in | Emojis do not count at all | Only the first person is to blame | It was fine because everyone did it
exercise: Why did Daniel feel so bad even at home? | The chat was on his phone wherever he went | His mum saw the photo and laughed at him too at dinner | He did not have a phone | The photo was only at school
exercise: What did Zack do to put it right? | Said sorry face to face and left the chat when it turned mean | Posted a nicer photo of Daniel | Deleted his emoji and said nothing | Said to Daniel that he was only copying the others in the chat
exercise: Ellie skipped homework for a game and nothing happened the first week. What did she wrongly think? | That she could always get away with it | That homework was finished | That the teacher had marked it already and given her a tick | That games were homework
exercise: What happened by the third week? | A note home and her parents called to school | She got a prize for gaming | Nothing ever happened, because teachers forget about it | The teacher stopped giving homework
exercise: Ellie did not speak to Mum all evening after the row. What put things right the next day? | Saying sorry and setting a homework-first timer | Waiting in her room until Mum came to apologise first | Asking for the game back at once | Saying the teacher was unfair
exercise: Why did Ellie's parents go to school? | To solve the problem together with the teacher | To tell Ellie off loudly in front of the whole class | To punish the teacher | To collect her game
exercise: Liam downloaded a game without asking. What happened? | It cost money and Mum was upset | It was free and Mum was really pleased | The tablet started working faster | Nothing happened to anyone at all
exercise: Mia pretended to be someone else online for fun. Someone believed it and got upset. What put it right? | Telling the truth and saying sorry | Making a second pretend account to explain things | Blocking the person | Saying it was obvious
exercise: Ben sent a scary chain message to his friends. A little girl got scared. What is true about chain messages? | Their threats are untrue, but they scare people | They come true if enough people send them on to friends | They are only scary for adults | They bring good luck if passed on
exercise: Ruby took a photo of her teacher and shared it. What was the consequence? | She had to see the head teacher | The teacher thanked her | She got a merit for her good photo of the classroom | Nothing, teachers don't mind
exercise: Noah told his friend's password to another boy. Someone logged in as his friend. What put it right? | Saying sorry and helping him change it | Telling him to make up a funnier password | Saying the other boy was the one to blame | Logging in himself to check it was all fine
exercise: Sam used Mum's account to buy a game skin. She saw the email. What is true? | Taking money online is still taking | Skins aren't real, so it's fine | It's fine to buy things if the account is already open | It's only wrong over $20
exercise: Ellie's friend says «I skipped homework all term and nothing happened». What does Ellie now know? | Getting away with it before doesn't mean it's safe or right | Homework is never checked | It's only wrong if your parents find out about it later | Teachers never call parents
exercise: Which is the bravest choice when a group chat turns mean about someone? | Leave the chat or tell an adult, and back them up | Stay quiet and keep reading | Add a funny joke to change the mood and move on | Screenshot it for later laughs
exercise: Ellie said «they called my parents in just to embarrass me». What is closer to the truth? | They came to sort out the problem together | The teacher only wanted to embarrass her in front of everyone | Parents are called in for every pupil | Parents like going to school
test: chat: «🤣🤣🤣 look at Dan's face». Everyone's adding laughs. What's right? | Don't add one; stand up for Dan or tell an adult | Add one, it's just a laugh | Add two laughing faces so you fit in with the rest | Send it to another chat
test: «I didn't post it, I only laughed, so it's not on me». What's true? | Laughing along is joining in | Only the person who posted is to blame | Emojis are invisible to everyone | It's fine if you didn't mean it
test: skipped homework for Fortnite last week, teacher didn't check, «it worked last time, so it's fine». Is it? | No, it will catch up with you, and it wasn't fine | Yes, homework doesn't count in winter | Yes, if she didn't check last week, she won't check now | Only on Fridays
test: Mum's text: «Miss Grant wants to see me and Dad on Thursday. Anything you want to tell me first?» Best reply? | Tell her the truth about the homework | «No idea what it's about» | «Miss Grant just hates me, she always picks on me» | Turn your phone off
test: «send this to 10 people or something bad happens tonight 👻». Your little cousin's scared. What do you tell her? | It's not true, don't send it, delete it | Send it quickly to be safe | Send it to 20 people, just to be extra safe tonight | Only send it to grown-ups
test: mate: «what's Jake's password? I just wanna see his messages». You know it. Best move? | Don't tell him; passwords are private | Tell him, but only if he promises not to tell Jake | Give him half of it | Log in for him instead
test: «sneaky pic of Mr Hall asleep in the staff room lol, posting it». What's right? | Don't post it; it's not yours to share | Post it, it's funny | Post it without his name so nobody gets in trouble | Post it for one hour
test: «made a fake account as a famous footballer, people think it's real». What should he do? | Delete it and tell people it was fake | Keep it, it's harmless fun and nobody gets hurt | Charge for autographs | Make another one
test: game skin's only £3, mum's account is logged in. Best move? | Ask her first | Buy it, she won't mind | Buy it straight away | Buy it with her card instead
test: After the meeting at school, Ellie's game is gone for a week and she slams her door. What's the wise way through? | Calm down, then say sorry and make a homework plan | Stay angry until she gets it back | Go and play the game at a friend's house instead | Tell the teacher she's unfair
test: Why might Daniel still feel bad at home, even though the photo was posted at school? | His phone brings the chat home with him | He doesn't have a phone | Photos only work at school | His mum posted the photo to the chat too
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the addition of an approving symbol to a derisive post is wrongful. | It constitutes participation in the derision | It is without effect upon the subject | Responsibility rests solely with the original author | Symbols are not a form of communication
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the summoning of parents to school in respect of incomplete homework? | It is a means of resolving the difficulty jointly and not of shaming the pupil | It is a penalty designed principally to humiliate the pupil before the class | It is undertaken for every pupil without cause | It relieves the pupil of responsibility
exam: It is given that a pupil omitted homework for one week without detection. Select the conclusion consistent with sound reasoning. | Prior non-detection does not establish that future omissions will pass or that they are right | Prior non-detection establishes a permanent exemption from the homework set | Homework is not subject to verification | Detection alone determines the obligation
exam: State the course of action most consistent with propriety upon learning another person's password. | The password is kept private and not used | The password is shared with trusted friends | The password is used only to read messages | The password is written down for reference
